Below is a **self‑contained Java example** that demonstrates:

1. The built‑in annotations (`@Override`, `@Deprecated`, `@SuppressWarnings`)  
2. A *custom* annotation with parameters, target, and runtime retention  
3. How to read those annotations at runtime with reflection  

```java
// File: AnnotationDemo.java
import java.lang.annotation.*;
import java.lang.reflect.*;

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 1.  BUILT‑IN ANNOTATIONS
// ------------------------------------------------------------

class BaseService {
    /** A method that will be overridden later */
    public void execute() {
        System.out.println("BaseService: executing");
    }
}

class DerivedService extends BaseService {
    /** The @Override annotation tells the compiler we intend
     *  to override a method from the superclass. */
    @Override
    public void execute() {
        System.out.println("DerivedService: overridden execution");
    }

    /** Mark this method as deprecated – the compiler will warn
     *  if it's used elsewhere. */
    @Deprecated
    public void oldMethod() {
        System.out.println("DerivedService: old method");
    }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 2.  CUSTOM ANNOTATION DEFINITION
// ------------------------------------------------------------

/**
 * Annotation used to mark methods that perform "critical" work.
 *
 * @author  YourName
 * @since   2026-10-09
 */
@Target(ElementType.METHOD)               // only on methods
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)        // available at runtime
public @interface Critical {
    String value() default "Unknown";       // optional parameter
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 3.  CLASS USING THE CUSTOM ANNOTATION
// ------------------------------------------------------------

class TaskRunner {
    @Critical("Initialization")
    public void init() {
        System.out.println("Initializing resources...");
    }

    @Critical("Processing")
    public void process() {
        System.out.println("Processing data...");
    }

    public void helper() {
        System.out.println("Helper method – not critical.");
    }
}

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// 4.  MAIN PROGRAM – DEMONSTRATE REFLECTION
// ------------------------------------------------------------

public class AnnotationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        System.out.println("=== Built‑in annotation demo ===");
        DerivedService ds = new DerivedService();
        ds.execute();                       // @Override
        ds.oldMethod();                     // @Deprecated

        System.out.println("\n=== Custom @Critical annotation demo ===");
        TaskRunner runner = new TaskRunner();
        Method[] methods = runner.getClass().getDeclaredMethods();

        for (Method m : methods) {
            if (m.isAnnotationPresent(Critical.class)) {
                Critical crit = m.getAnnotation(Critical.class);
                System.out.printf("Method '%s' is CRITICAL: %s%n",
                        m.getName(), crit.value());
            }
        }

        // Example of @SuppressWarnings
        @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
        java.util.List rawList = new java.util.ArrayList(); // raw type warning suppressed
        rawList.add("hello");
        System.out.println("\nSuppressWarnings demo: rawList = " + rawList);
    }
}
```

### What the program does

| Section | What it shows |
|---------|---------------|
| **Built‑in annotations** | `@Override` (compiler checks), `@Deprecated` (compiler warns), `@SuppressWarnings` (runtime suppression) |
| **Custom annotation** | `@Critical` is defined with a parameter (`value`), targeted at methods, retained at runtime. |
| **Reflection** | In `main`, we iterate over the methods of `TaskRunner`, look for `@Critical`, and print its parameter. |
| **Output** | Running the program prints messages that highlight the overridden method, the deprecated warning (shown by the compiler), and which methods are marked as critical. |

> **Tip**: Compile with `-Xlint:deprecation` to see the deprecation warning for `oldMethod`.

Feel free to copy the code into a single file named `AnnotationDemo.java`, compile with `javac AnnotationDemo.java`, and run with `java AnnotationDemo`. The output will demonstrate all the annotations in action.
